{"offset": 0, "data": [{"citingPaper": {"paperId": "cda1c495f0ef51c79c1cb1745c6f9cb642a7bd46", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.34994", "CorpusId": 294366549}, "title": "Why Are LLMs Confidently Wrong? Correcting Overconfident Errors via Causal Head Intervention", "venue": "", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34994, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "2374313058", "name": "Jing Ren"}, {"authorId": "2132475886", "name": "Bo-Wen Li"}, {"authorId": "2358600235", "name": "Zi-Qi Xu"}, {"authorId": "2274914403", "name": "Xue-Chao Yang"}, {"authorId": "2366078735", "name": "Feng Xia"}], "abstract": "Modern large language models (LLMs) often exhibit overconfident errors, assigning high confidence to incorrect predictions and thereby undermining reliability in real-world use. Existing calibration methods typically rely on post-hoc adjustment or retraining, operating at the output level without addressing the underlying causes of miscalibration. We propose CoCHI, (Class-oriented Causal Head Intervention), a framework that improves calibration by identifying and intervening on internal components responsible for distinct confidence behaviours. By analysing predictions through correctness-confidence patterns, CoCHI enables targeted intervention on model internals, reducing overconfident errors while preserving correct predictions. Our approach provides a mechanistic perspective on calibration, linking reliability to identifiable structures within the model rather than treating it as an output-level issue. Experiments across multiple LLMs show consistent improvements in calibration metrics, including Expected Calibration Error, Negative Log-Likelihood, and Brier Score, while maintaining accuracy on five multiple-choice question answering benchmarks. These results suggest that effective calibration requires not only adjusting outputs, but also addressing the underlying causal mechanisms within the model."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "dd40e11bd6dcb492bc55b22168b6473e430be12a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22046", "CorpusId": 293003633}, "title": "Effectiveness of Hidden-State Probes for Localizing Reasoning Failures", "venue": "", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22046, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "2500606166", "name": "Shubham Subhnil"}, {"authorId": "2330190108", "name": "Yannis Belkhiter"}, {"authorId": "2278648904", "name": "Erik Miehling"}, {"authorId": "2059761581", "name": "Adam Dahlgren Lindstr\u00f6m"}, {"authorId": "2253674008", "name": "Elizabeth M. Daly"}], "abstract": "Verifying the final answer of a long reasoning trace may show that the trace is wrong but it does not distinguish three cases. The problem may lie beyond the model's capability, another attempt would succeed, or one step in an otherwise recoverable trace made the trace fail. Standard hidden-state probes are trained on final-answer correctness, a label that conflates these cases. Monte Carlo rollouts distinguish the cases and locate the failing step at additional inference cost. We test the hidden states of 4B and 8B reasoning models for the failure type and the failing step and compare each probe with the strongest baseline that uses no hidden states. On incorrect 8B traces, the confidence probe, a calibrated correctness probe at a midpoint cutoff, misses 81% of cases in which a single step makes the trace unrecoverable and 47% of cases in which another attempt would succeed. A probe with a different training design over the same states reverses the ordering of these two kinds of failure, so either ordering can be read from those states. At both scales, no single tested probe locates the failing step more accurately than a rule based only on trace length, and at 4B a probe trained to predict that step fails to outperform this rule in any of 10 runs on identical traces. The confidence probe therefore indicates that an answer is wrong, but its failure-type ordering depends on its training design."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a7a148c1365f1b7033b04f08b48e00c0d01247e4", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22636", "CorpusId": 293453090}, "title": "Measuring Mechanistic Uncertainty in Large Language Models via Causal Robustness", "venue": "", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22636, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "2131100440", "name": "Aneesh Komanduri"}, {"authorId": "2243245481", "name": "Majid Afshar"}, {"authorId": "2262214824", "name": "Timothy A. Miller"}, {"authorId": "2145972668", "name": "Yan-Jun Gao"}], "abstract": "Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used in critical domains, where their susceptibility to hallucinations makes reliable and interpretable uncertainty quantification essential. We introduce the concept of mechanistic uncertainty, which characterizes uncertainty through the stability of the internal mechanisms supporting a model's response. We propose the causal robustness margin, defined as the minimum internal intervention required to deviate the model's output, and operationalize it using Jacobian Lens (Jlens) concept steering vectors. Across three open-source models and five question-answering datasets, we show incorrect and uncertain responses exhibit smaller margins, indicating greater sensitivity to internal interventions, and the causal robustness margin complements conventional output-based uncertainty methods. Finally, in a controlled knowledge injection experiment, correctly learned facts show increased margins after training, suggesting that causal robustness provides a mechanistically grounded signal of uncertainty."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f90439c097ac720ac6b2880ac64202791c713054", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "conf/jcdl/SiravuriAA26", "DOI": "10.1145/3805696.3846034", "CorpusId": 292831434}, "title": "Triaging Defects in Scholarly Result Graphs with Extraction, Evidence, and Consistency Signals", "venue": "ACM/IEEE Joint Conference on Digital Libraries", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1145/3805696.3846034", "status": "HYBRID", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1145/3805696.3846034?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1145/3805696.3846034, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "19287335", "name": "Harish Varma Siravuri"}, {"authorId": "46259575", "name": "Akhil Pandey Akella"}, {"authorId": "2122345729", "name": "Hamed Alhoori"}], "abstract": "With the growing volume of scientific literature, scholarly knowledge graphs are increasingly used to organize research findings, and large language models now build such graphs automatically. However, the facts extracted by these methods are often wrong. Since most facts cannot be compared against an external reference, users cannot tell which ones need rechecking. In this study we propose a way to flag such errors. We observed that a scholarly result graph contains several distinct classes of defect, and that detecting them calls for different kinds of evidence. A human audit shows that most audited disagreements are not extraction errors but correct values reported under a different experimental setting, which we separate from genuine source-extraction errors. We compare three signals, namely a value-blind extraction-reliability signal drawn from how a fact was extracted, graph-consistency checks over the reference structure, and a value-aware result-validity signal in which a model reads each stored value against its cited evidence. No single signal detects every kind of defect. Graph-consistency checks best separate disagreements with the reference. On a score-stratified human sample, the value-aware check best detects genuine extraction errors, raising their detection from an AUROC of 0.70 to 0.95; these are sample-conditional rankings, not graph-wide estimates. Each signal works best on a different target, while a combined score gives one triage ranking across both. We also tested whether flagged errors could be corrected automatically. This proved unsafe, since about 25 percent of the proposed corrections would overwrite a correct fact, so the tool prioritizes errors for human review rather than editing the graph. We release the error-scored graph, a benchmark with a 150-fact human-audited error breakdown, and the human validation set."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "2cc675743b69d9f24b2a319fa7d0ecc19deb3421", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.33220", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-33220", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.33220", "CorpusId": 292433942}, "title": "When Do Models Admit They Are Wrong? Failure Disclosure Is Unstable Under Reinforcement Learning", "venue": "arXiv.org", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.33220, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "144318169", "name": "Steven Y. Feng"}, {"authorId": "2251316895", "name": "Noah D. Goodman"}, {"authorId": "2287129670", "name": "Michael C. Frank"}, {"authorId": "146614650", "name": "Evan Hubinger"}, {"authorId": "1698286", "name": "P. Bogdan"}, {"authorId": "32322945", "name": "Andrew Kyle Lampinen"}], "abstract": "Outcome-based reinforcement learning can produce models with similar task performance but very different ways of communicating about their mistakes. We study failure disclosure: whether a model admits that an attempted solution failed rather than staying silent or presenting it as successful. Across repeated outcome-only GRPO training runs, failure disclosure varies far more than task accuracy. The pattern extends to a second reasoning task and stabilized PPO, persists at 7B, and also appears in an instruction-conditioned 32B setting. We also find that small floating-point and sampling differences during training can redirect reporting behavior even when the task objective and earlier training history are held fixed. Additional tests show that failure disclosure is not a single decision: Checking the answer, entering a report, and completing the admission can separate, and the weak point depends on the task and response format. Further, experiments with neutral controls show more broadly that behaviors left weakly constrained by training are especially likely to vary across runs, of which failure disclosure is an example. We can reduce variability in failure disclosure by discouraging the model from drifting from its starting policy on failed, well-formed responses. This makes reporting substantially more consistent, though its effect on task performance depends on the setting. Stable task accuracy therefore does not guarantee stable safety-relevant behavior: Researchers should measure these behaviors directly across runs and design training methods that keep them reliable."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3da7f3e712e096a91cdcd19ae215eb3eba90a69f", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2606-03969", "ArXiv": "2606.03969", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2606.03969", "CorpusId": 288903773}, "title": "Quantifying Faithful Confidence Expression in Large Reasoning Models", "venue": "arXiv.org", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.03969, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "2114282496", "name": "Areeb Gani"}, {"authorId": "2375142690", "name": "Asal Meskin"}, {"authorId": "2383414185", "name": "G. K. Liu"}, {"authorId": "2329556076", "name": "Arman Cohan"}], "abstract": "Reliable uncertainty communication is critical to the trustworthiness of LLMs, yet faithful calibration (FC)--the alignment between models'intrinsic and (linguistically) expressed confidence--is a persistent failure mode. This challenge is key for large reasoning models (LRMs), whose extended reasoning traces are often interpreted by users as evidence of deliberation, competence, and confidence. Despite the importance of FC and wide usage of LRMs, the extent to which LRMs can faithfully express their confidence remains poorly understood. Moreover, the prevailing paradigm to measure FC does not generalize well to the long chain-of-thought outputs generated by LRMs, which tend to lack clear step boundaries, involve inconsistent step structure, and encode complex conditional dependencies throughout the trace--complicating estimation of intrinsic confidence. To address this challenge, we introduce a novel framework to systematically quantify FC of LRMs. Our framework analyzes linguistic decisiveness relative to three sources of internal uncertainty, based on token probabilities, hidden states, and sampled response consistency. We also devise a prefix-conditioned sampling approach to control for conditional and structural variation across traces. Applying our framework to a diverse suite of leading models, datasets, and prompts, we find that faithful confidence expression is a significant challenge for LRMs. Reasoning behaviors do not automatically translate to improved FC, and prompt interventions for non-reasoning models do not improve faithfulness in the reasoning setting. Different confidence estimators further produce divergent assessments of the same traces, revealing fragility in prior evaluation methodologies. Taken together, our work establishes FC as a distinct reliability and alignment target for LRMs, particularly as such systems are increasingly deployed in high-stakes contexts."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "795f52f6c88717ae59173a06fe4de92d6ca288e9", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2604-24070", "ArXiv": "2604.24070", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2604.24070", "CorpusId": 287782449}, "title": "Distilling Self-Consistency into Verbal Confidence: A Pre-Registered Negative Result and Post-Hoc Rescue on Gemma 3 4B", "venue": "arXiv.org", "year": 2026, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.24070, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "6326414", "name": "Jon-Paul Cacioli"}], "abstract": "Small instruct-tuned LLMs produce degenerate verbal confidence under minimal elicitation: ceiling rates above 95%, near-chance Type-2 AUROC, and Invalid validity profiles. We test whether confidence-conditioned supervised fine-tuning (CSFT) with self-consistency-derived targets can close the gap between internal information and verbal readout. A pre-registered Phase 0 protocol on Gemma 3 4B-it with a modal filter restricting training to items with correct modal answers produced a negative result: AUROC2 dropped from 0.554 to 0.509 due to label-entropy collapse in the training targets. An exploratory rescue removed the filter, training on all 2,000 calibration items. This produced a binary verbal correctness discriminator with AUROC2 = 0.774 on held-out TriviaQA, compressing a 10-sample self-consistency signal (AUROC2 = 0.999) into a single-pass readout exceeding logit entropy (0.701). The shuffled-target control showed no improvement (0.501). On MMLU, accuracy improved from 54.2% to 77.4% with the shuffled model at baseline (56.1%), supporting a target-dependent interpretation. The result is exploratory, binary rather than continuously calibrated, and observed at a single scale. It identifies two design lessons: confidence training requires label entropy, and correct targets regularise output format."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ae5dbc313f752a4084cdadc7d5708fc99c567963", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2408.13378", "CorpusId": 289934714}, "title": "DrugAgent: Reliable Multi-Agent Integration of Conflicting Biomedical Evidence for Drug-Target Interaction Assessment", "venue": "", "year": 2024, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.13378, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "authors": [{"authorId": "2301432341", "name": "Yoshitaka Inoue"}, {"authorId": "2317014671", "name": "Tianci Song"}, {"authorId": "2244251890", "name": "Xinlin Wang"}, {"authorId": "1807167", "name": "R. Kuang"}, {"authorId": "2304553065", "name": "Tian-Fan Fu"}, {"authorId": "2301312578", "name": "Augustin Luna"}], "abstract": "Workflows in drug-target interaction (DTI) assessment require integrating heterogeneous data from predictive models, curated resources, and observations from experimental literature. This evidence can be incomplete or conflicting. DrugAgent is a large language model (LLM)-based multi-agent system focused on DTI evidence integration that integrates outputs from machine learning, knowledge graph, and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) agents. DrugAgent converts agent outputs into interpretable representations, then summarizes conflict across the evidence. We evaluated DrugAgent on kinase screening data of 900 pairs spanning 178 kinases and 42 inhibitors, and an androgen receptor antagonist screening benchmark. On the kinase dataset, LLM-as-a-Judge evaluation indicated outputs were faithful to input evidence in 98.8% of cases. Biological plausibility of returned summarization was high (scores 3-4 out of 5) across ground-truth classes: 79% of Weak activity labels cases (81% for Moderate/77% Strong); Strong cases received higher scores than Weak/Moderate. Label stability showed 98% agreement across runs. Results on the antagonist benchmark were consistent with the kinase dataset. Retrieved literature provided the greatest benefit when direct drug-target evidence was available, highlighting the importance of evidence availability for RAG-based integration. DrugAgent provides heterogeneous evidence-grounded DTI assessment, complementing standalone DTI prediction. We provide strategies to model agreement, conflict, and uncertainty in biomedical evidence integration. Code: https://github.com/sciluna/DrugAgent."}}]}
